{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Final evaluation — commit 24917e843b1472adc682bcde7a9c2e5a1727acfd\n\nOverall verdict: unusually mature and well-governed for a project of this size. The dominant risks are localized complexity, incomplete type precision, and small documentation/test-environment truth gaps—not systemic correctness or testing weakness.\n\nRatings:\n- Code quality: 7.5/10\n- Architectural quality: 8.0/10\n- Documentation quality: 8.0/10\n- Test quality: 8.5/10\n\n### Verification and measurements\n- Ruff check + format-check passed across 1,831 files.\n- Complexity ratchet passed: active=68, new=0, increased=0, stale=0.\n- Mypy passed all 440 source modules.\n- Zizmor, actionlint, documentation index/internal-link checks passed.\n- Source inventory: 441 Python files, 131,246 LOC, median 229 LOC.\n- Test inventory: 1,249 Python files, 265,045 LOC, 9,748 statically defined test functions: 7,565 unit; 1,333 interface/contract; 599 scripts; 158 integration; 81 external; 12 E2E.\n- Canonical default suite after locked dependency sync, outside the socket-restricted sandbox: 11,480 passed, 19 skipped, 2 failed in 13m54s. Coverage was 83.86% combined, 85.77% statements, 78.19% branches.\n- Integration lane: 138 passed, 1 skipped in 2m38s.\n- No literal assert True tests found. Only 11 tests had exclusively weak local is-not-None-style assertions; sampled cases generally had meaningful surrounding behavioral setup/oracles.\n\n### Strengths\n1. Code/governance: shrink-only module-size, complexity, and typing ratchets; broad blocking Ruff/mypy/format gates; explicit environment/bootstrap policy; strong error and concurrency documentation.\n2. Architecture: event ownership and adapters are clearly explained; CLI, library, and MCP share a core; selected high-risk seams have executable negative controls. The Jira-family boundary test includes synthetic forbidden edges, so it cannot pass vacuously.\n3. Documentation: audience-oriented index, architecture + ADR corpus, generated CLI/MCP/env references, README golden path, internal-link/index gates, and roughly 1% measured duplicate lines. Context is generally layered rather than repeated.\n4. Tests: unusually strong tiering and hygiene infrastructure. tests/conftest.py:95+ confines external markers and tests/conftest.py:149+ blocks accidental sockets; tests/interfaces/conftest.py:40 builds one isolated reusable store template per xdist worker; tests/external/conftest.py:121 fails an all-skipped live lane and :143 requires explicit opt-in. The weekly external matrix covers Anthropic, Bedrock, OpenAI, Jira Cloud, Jira Data Center, and Langfuse. Existing mutation documentation distinguishes behavioral tests from structural tripwires/change detectors and records 99–100% mapped scores on selected cores.\n\n### Findings and remedies\nMEDIUM-HIGH — Complexity/file-size debt. 87 source modules exceed the documented 500-LOC target (docs/architecture.md:299); llm/runner.py, _config_schema.py, and _commands/init.py are exactly 800 LOC, _ref_lock.py is 799, and signing.py is 798. Independent lizard analysis found 206 functions above complexity 15. Representative hotspots are compute() in src/rebar/_engine_support/next_batch.py:96, which combines resolution, replay, BFS, tombstones, dependency readiness, parent policy, and greedy scheduling, and import_tickets() in src/rebar/_io/import_ndjson.py:118, a five-pass importer in one long orchestration function. Continue the existing shrink-only ratchets, prioritizing high-complexity orchestration and near-cap files; split only at current call-graph seams. The ratchet itself is a major strength and already prevents regression.\n\nMEDIUM — Broad but shallow typing. Normal mypy passes all source, but a diagnostic global --disallow-untyped-defs run found 687 errors. pyproject.toml:462 globally ignores missing imports and only rebar.graph.* / rebar.grounding.* currently disallow untyped definitions (:504). Expand package-by-package strictness and narrow ignore_missing_imports at transport boundaries. Existing open ticket 06e0-fb68-657a-4ea1 tracks this direction.\n\nMEDIUM — Architectural enforcement is selective rather than systemic. High-risk seams have good bespoke checks, but there is no small repository-wide package dependency/DAG contract, so new cross-layer imports outside protected families rely on review. Define stable layer contracts with positive and synthetic-negative controls while preserving facade/lazy-import exceptions. Follow-up: 44ea-43c6-b351-4d81.\n\nMEDIUM — Assignee examples contradict reconciler behavior. README.md:44 and docs/user-guide.md:115 teach --assignee alice, while docs/config.md:431 requires an email/accountId and explains that a bare handle is left unassigned. Omit the option in quickstarts or use a clearly Jira-resolvable value. Included in 879f-5549-91e3-4d6f.\n\nLOW — Documentation/navigation drift. docs/README.md:25 routes “Drive rebar over MCP” to llm-framework.md rather than mcp-reference.md; docs/README.md:91 describes removed .rebar/config.conf instead of rebar.toml/[tool.rebar]. .github/workflows/_optionality.yml:74+ still says core has only PyYAML and describes jsonschema as absent although the current dependency topology differs. pyproject.toml:457+ calls non-core mypy advisory despite the whole-library CI gate. Fix the source text and protect stable truths with deterministic drift checks. Follow-up: 879f-5549-91e3-4d6f.\n\nLOW-MEDIUM — Mutation evidence is strong but stale/non-gating. docs/mutation-testing.md:92 and :110 date the recorded scores to June/July 2026, and no workflow invokes mutmut. Add a bounded scheduled/manual lane for only the already selected cores, failing on score regression/new survivors. Follow-up: 521b-04c3-572f-4514.\n\nLOW — Two reproducible test-environment assumptions. tests/interfaces/contracts/test_help_overview_coverage.py:142 recursively decodes every file under src/rebar as UTF-8, so an ignored Mach-O esbuild binary under editor_assets/node_modules fails the “shipped surfaces” check. tests/unit/test_attestation_signing.py:430 clones the audit checkout and fails when that checkout is a shallow promisor clone missing historical objects. The first test should enumerate tracked/package-shipped textual inputs; the second should use a self-contained Git fixture or explicitly handle partial checkouts. Both should retain negative controls so their behavioral oracles remain strong. Follow-up: def1-9c3c-6802-48bd.\n\n### Limits\nNo live third-party calls were made because provider/Jira credentials were not in scope; external-integration quality is assessed from the tests, explicit opt-in/all-skipped controls, and scheduled workflow matrix rather than a live run. The independent complexity scan is diagnostic; the checked-in shrink-only ratchet is the authoritative gate. No production or test files were modified.\n\nRelated prior audit: 1d3c-547d-f0cf-426c."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786212796629918001,"uuid":"d86fd86f-ad2b-4a97-8bb5-8774ac4fbdd7"}